---
import BaseLayout from "../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Header from "../../../../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../../../../components/Footer.astro";
import CaseStudyHero from "../../../../components/CaseStudyHero.astro";
import { getPaymentCaseStudy } from "../../../../data/paymentCaseStudies";
import "../../../../styles/growth-platform.css";
import "../../../../styles/case-studies.css";

const slug = "oxxo-pay-cash-vouchers-time-limited-inventory";
const study = getPaymentCaseStudy(slug);
if (!study) throw new Error(`Case study not found: ${slug}`);

const site = "https://matteoarellano.com";
const hub = `${site}/payment-solutions/case-studies/`;
const canonical = `${hub}${slug}/`;
const title = "How to Prevent Failed Orders with OXXO Pay Cash Payments | Case Study";
const description = study.summary;

const publishedLabel = new Date(`${study.published}T00:00:00Z`).toLocaleDateString("en-GB", {
  day: "numeric",
  month: "long",
  year: "numeric",
  timeZone: "UTC",
});

const contents = [
  { id: "why", label: "Why companies offer OXXO Pay" },
  { id: "how", label: "How an OXXO Pay payment works" },
  { id: "example", label: "The example: tickets with a time limit" },
  { id: "problems", label: "Five problems" },
  { id: "solutions", label: "Seven steps to solve them" },
  { id: "root-cause", label: "The root cause and three long-term options" },
  { id: "conclusion", label: "Conclusion" },
];

const paymentSteps = [
  "The customer places an order online and chooses OXXO Pay.",
  "The website shows a payment reference: a number of 14 to 16 digits, or a barcode. The reference has an expiry date.",
  "The customer goes to an OXXO store and pays the amount in cash at the till.",
  "The payment provider sends a payment confirmation to the website.",
  "The website completes the order. The money arrives in the company's bank account some days later.",
];

const facts = [
  { label: "Maximum amount per payment", value: "MXN 10,000" },
  { label: "Fee paid by the customer at the store", value: "MXN 17 with OXXO's own service" },
  { label: "Fee paid by the business", value: "From 3.9% + VAT with OXXO's own service; different with other providers" },
  {
    label: "How long a payment reference is valid",
    value: "Set by the provider and the business: 1 to 7 days with Stripe, 5 days on some platforms, 30 days by default on others",
  },
  { label: "Payment confirmation", value: "Almost immediate with some providers; by the next business day with Stripe" },
  { label: "Refunds and chargebacks", value: "Stripe does not support refunds; customers cannot open a chargeback" },
];

const problems = [
  {
    title: "The payment reference is valid for longer than the reservation",
    body: [
      "The payment provider sets the expiry date of the reference. The venue sets the end of the reservation. If the reference is valid for longer than the reservation, the customer can pay for seats that the venue has already released or sold to someone else.",
      "Making the reservation as long as the reference does not solve this. The seats then stay blocked for days, and many references are never paid. In the work that this case study is based on, many customers chose to pay in cash. For this reason, the difference between the two time limits caused problems often, not only in rare cases.",
    ],
    next: "The most serious result of this difference is the second problem.",
  },
  {
    title: "The customer pays, but the product cannot be issued",
    body: [
      "The customer pays at the store, and the website receives the payment confirmation. However, the ticket cannot be issued, because the reservation has ended, the seats were sold, or the venue's system failed. The customer did everything correctly and has a receipt from the store, but no ticket.",
    ],
    next: "In this situation, the business must return the money. This is more difficult than it sounds.",
  },
  {
    title: "Cash payments are difficult to return",
    body: [
      "The product cannot be refunded, so there is no standard refund process. The payment method does not help either. Stripe, for example, does not support refunds for OXXO payments, and asks businesses to create their own process to return money to customers.",
      "Customers also cannot open a chargeback (a payment dispute through their bank), so every case goes to the business's customer service team. In addition, many customers who pay in cash do not have a bank account where the business can send the money.",
    ],
    next: "To solve these cases quickly, the business needs to know exactly when a payment arrives. This leads to the fourth problem.",
  },
  {
    title: "Payment confirmations can arrive late, without a clear reason",
    body: [
      "The payment confirmation arrives as a webhook: an automatic message from the payment provider to the website. How long this takes depends on the provider. Some providers confirm almost immediately; Stripe confirms by the next business day.",
      "When a confirmation is late, the business cannot see the difference between \"the customer has not paid yet\" and \"the customer has paid, but the confirmation has not arrived\". In the work that this case study is based on, the provider gave little information about the status of its confirmations. There was no clear signal when confirmations were late or failing, so the team often learned about problems from customer complaints.",
    ],
    next: "The last problem is smaller, but customers notice it.",
  },
  {
    title: "The customer pays an extra fee at the store",
    body: [
      "At the store, the customer pays a fixed fee in addition to the price of the product: MXN 17 with OXXO's own service. For a low-priced ticket, this is a large percentage of the price. It is also paid by the customers who have the fewest other ways to pay.",
    ],
    next: "These five problems are connected, so the solutions are connected too. The next section describes seven practical steps.",
  },
];

const solutions = [
  {
    title: "Calculate the payment deadline from the reservation",
    body: "For each order, set the expiry of the payment reference to the end of the reservation, minus a safety margin. If the reservation is too short for a customer to visit a store, do not offer cash for that product. Alternatively, ask the company that owns the inventory for a longer reservation for cash orders.",
  },
  {
    title: "Show the deadline clearly",
    body: "Show the exact date, time and time zone of the deadline. For example, Stripe references expire at 23:59 Mexico City time. Explain clearly that the reference is not a ticket, and that the order is complete only after the payment is confirmed.",
  },
  {
    title: "Check availability before you issue the product",
    body: "When the payment confirmation arrives, check that the seats are still reserved before you issue the ticket. If they are not, apply a solution that you agreed in advance: similar seats, a credit or a refund.",
  },
  {
    title: "Prepare a refund process before launch",
    body: "The payment provider will not return cash payments for you. Before you launch, decide how you will return money: a credit on your website, a bank transfer through SPEI, or a payment to a digital wallet. Also decide who approves each refund.",
  },
  {
    title: "Do not depend only on payment confirmations",
    body: "Process each confirmation only once, and verify that it really comes from the provider. Ask the provider regularly about references that will expire soon. Create an alert when no payments arrive during hours when payments normally arrive. Every day, compare the payments you received with your orders.",
  },
  {
    title: "Measure each step of the cash payment process",
    body: "Track how many references are created, how many are paid, how many are paid before the deadline, how many orders are completed automatically and how many need manual help. Track \"paid but not completed\" as a separate number, and make one person responsible for it.",
  },
  {
    title: "Show the store fee before the customer chooses cash",
    body: "Show the fee before the customer selects OXXO Pay. For low-priced products, consider paying the fee for the customer.",
  },
];

const options = [
  {
    name: "Instant bank payments (SPEI, CoDi, DiMo)",
    helps: "Customers with a bank account",
    speed: "A few seconds",
    cost: "Few people use CoDi and DiMo today",
  },
  {
    name: "Cash added to a digital wallet first",
    helps: "Customers without a bank account",
    speed: "A few seconds, after the cash deposit",
    cost: "The customer needs an app and pays a deposit fee",
  },
  {
    name: "A longer reservation, for a fee",
    helps: "All customers who pay in cash",
    speed: "No change: the customer still pays at a store",
    cost: "A fee for the customer; the inventory owner must agree",
  },
];

const sources = [
  { name: "Stripe Docs — OXXO payments", url: "https://docs.stripe.com/payments/oxxo" },
  {
    name: "Stripe Docs — Pagos con OXXO (expiry and confirmation time)",
    url: "https://docs.stripe.com/payments/oxxo?locale=es-419",
  },
  {
    name: "Tiendanube Help Center — Cash payments in Pago Nube (January 2026)",
    url: "https://ayuda.tiendanube.com/es_MX/pago-nube-mx/como-funcionan-los-pagos-con-efectivo-en-pago-nube",
  },
  {
    name: "Tiendanube — OXXO Pay app listing (fees)",
    url: "https://www.tiendanube.com/mx/tienda-aplicaciones-nube/digitalfemsa",
  },
  {
    name: "Tiendanube — ¿Qué es Oxxo Pay y cómo usarlo?",
    url: "https://www.tiendanube.com/blog/como-cobrar-en-oxxo/",
  },
  {
    name: "Conekta Docs — Webhooks (including the OXXO order-expired event)",
    url: "https://developers.conekta.com/resources/webhooks",
  },
  { name: "Forbes México — cash use in the ENIF 2024", url: "https://forbes.com.mx/?p=1357170" },
  {
    name: "PaynoPain — Payment methods in Mexico (ENIF 2024 figures)",
    url: "https://paynopain.com/en/blog/payment-methods-mexico-2025-trends-and-consumer-habits/",
  },
  {
    name: "The Paypers — Mexico: 2024 analysis of payments and ecommerce trends",
    url: "https://thepaypers.com/payments/expert-views/mexico-2024-analysis-of-payments-and-ecommerce-trends",
  },
  { name: "DPL News — SPEI at 22 years", url: "https://dplnews.com/?p=326663" },
  { name: "DPL News — Se acerca el SPEI 2.0 (CoDi and DiMo use)", url: "https://dplnews.com/?p=290343" },
  {
    name: "Líder Empresarial — Banxico standardises mobile transfers",
    url: "https://www.liderempresarial.com/banxico-obliga-a-la-banca-a-simplificar-las-transferencias-moviles/",
  },
  { name: "Descubre — Spin by OXXO company profile", url: "https://www.descubre.vc/spin" },
  {
    name: "Volaris — Payment options and paid reservation",
    url: "https://cms.volaris.com/es/informacion-util/opciones-de-pago/",
  },
];

const structuredData = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      "@id": `${canonical}#article`,
      headline: study.title,
      description,
      url: canonical,
      mainEntityOfPage: canonical,
      inLanguage: "en",
      datePublished: study.published,
      dateModified: study.updated ?? study.published,
      author: { "@id": `${site}/#person` },
      publisher: { "@id": `${site}/#person` },
      image: `${site}/images/case-studies/lofoten-coast-og.jpg`,
      isPartOf: { "@id": `${hub}#page` },
      about: [
        { "@type": "Thing", name: "OXXO Pay" },
        { "@type": "Thing", name: "Cash voucher payments" },
        { "@type": "Thing", name: "Payment webhooks" },
      ],
      spatialCoverage: { "@type": "Country", name: study.country },
      keywords: study.technologies.join(", "),
      citation: sources.map((source) => source.url),
    },
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      "@id": `${canonical}#breadcrumb`,
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: `${site}/` },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Payment solutions", item: `${site}/payment-solutions/` },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Case studies", item: hub },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 4, name: study.title, item: canonical },
      ],
    },
  ],
};

const emailHref = `mailto:matteo.visibility@gmail.com?subject=${encodeURIComponent(
  "Cash payments (OXXO Pay)",
)}`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  image="/images/case-studies/lofoten-coast-og.jpg"
>
  <Header />

  <script is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(structuredData)} />

  <main class="cs-page">
    <CaseStudyHero
      crumbLabel="Case studies"
      crumbHref="/payment-solutions/case-studies/"
      title={study.title}
      titleId="article-title"
      variant="article"
    >
      {study.region} · {study.country} · <time datetime={study.published}>{publishedLabel}</time> ·
      {study.readingMinutes} min read
    </CaseStudyHero>

    <article class="cs-article" aria-labelledby="article-title">
      <p class="cs-dek">
        OXXO Pay lets customers in Mexico pay for online orders in cash at a
        convenience store. For most products, this works well. For products
        that are only reserved for a limited time, such as tickets or bookings,
        it can lead to failed orders. This case study explains why this happens
        and what you can do about it.
      </p>

      <dl class="cs-facts-line">
        <div><dt>Region</dt><dd>{study.region} · {study.country}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Topics</dt><dd>{study.technologies.join(", ")}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>My role</dt><dd>{study.role}</dd></div>
      </dl>

      <aside class="cs-note" aria-label="About this case study">
        <strong>About this case study</strong>
        <p>
          This case study is based on my work on the integration of OXXO Pay
          for a company that I do not name. To protect that company, the
          example in this article is <em>{study.illustration}</em>. The real
          company is in a <strong>different industry</strong>. The example has
          the same three characteristics that caused the problems: the product
          is reserved for a limited time, another company issues the final
          product, and the product cannot be refunded. The article contains no
          client names, figures or internal details. All market data comes
          from public sources, listed at the end.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <section class="cs-summary" aria-labelledby="summary-title">
        <h2 id="summary-title">Summary</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Many customers in Mexico pay in cash, so OXXO Pay can increase sales.</li>
          <li>
            Problems start when the payment deadline is later than the
            reservation deadline. Customers can then pay for a product that is
            no longer reserved for them.
          </li>
          <li>
            Cash payments are difficult to return. For example, Stripe does not
            support refunds for OXXO payments.
          </li>
          <li>
            To reduce the problems: calculate the payment deadline from the
            reservation, check availability before you issue the product,
            prepare a refund process and monitor payment confirmations.
          </li>
          <li>
            To remove the cause, reduce the time between the order and the
            payment: instant bank payments, cash added to a digital wallet
            first, or a longer reservation for a fee.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <nav class="cs-toc" aria-labelledby="toc-title">
        <h2 id="toc-title">In this article</h2>
        <ol>
          {contents.map((item) => <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>)}
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <!-- 1 -->
      <section id="why" aria-labelledby="why-title">
        <h2 id="why-title">Why companies offer OXXO Pay</h2>
        <p>
          Cash is still the most common way to pay for larger purchases in
          Mexico. According to the 2024 National Survey of Financial Inclusion
          (ENIF), 73.5% of purchases of MXN 501 or more were paid in cash.
          About three in four adults have at least one formal financial
          product. This means that about one in four adults has none, and many
          people with a bank account still prefer to pay in cash.
        </p>
        <p>
          OXXO connects these customers with online shops. It has more than
          20,000 convenience stores in Mexico, and in 2024 it processed about
          half of all voucher and cash payments in Mexican online commerce. A
          company that does not offer a cash option can lose these customers.
        </p>
        <p class="cs-bridge">
          To see where the problems start, it is useful to look at how an OXXO
          Pay payment works, step by step.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 2 -->
      <section id="how" aria-labelledby="how-title">
        <h2 id="how-title">How an OXXO Pay payment works</h2>
        <ol class="cs-flow">
          {
            paymentSteps.map((step, index) => (
              <li class:list={[index === 2 && "is-key"]}>
                <span class="cs-flow-number">{index + 1}</span>
                <p>{step}</p>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>

        <figure class="cs-facts" aria-labelledby="facts-title">
          <figcaption id="facts-title">OXXO Pay: key facts</figcaption>
          <dl>
            {
              facts.map((fact) => (
                <div>
                  <dt>{fact.label}</dt>
                  <dd>{fact.value}</dd>
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </dl>
        </figure>

        <p class="cs-bridge">
          Step 3 is the important one. Between step 2 and step 3, time passes:
          minutes, hours or days. The customer decides when to pay. For a
          product that can wait in a warehouse, this delay is not a problem.
          For a product that is only reserved for a limited time, it is. The
          next section uses an example to show why.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 3 -->
      <section id="example" aria-labelledby="example-title">
        <h2 id="example-title">The example: tickets with a time limit</h2>
        <p>
          In this example, a marketplace sells concert tickets for several
          venues. When a customer selects seats, the marketplace asks the
          venue's ticket system to reserve them. The venue decides how long the
          reservation lasts, for example 20 minutes or a few hours, and the
          marketplace cannot change it. Only the venue's system can issue the
          ticket, and tickets cannot be refunded.
        </p>
        <p>
          With a card payment, this works well. The bank approves the payment
          in a few seconds, so the customer pays while the seats are still
          reserved. With OXXO Pay, the customer receives a reference and pays
          later, at a store. As a result, there are now two time limits, set by
          two different systems.
        </p>

        <figure class="cs-clocks" aria-labelledby="clocks-title">
          <figcaption id="clocks-title">Two time limits <span>(example values)</span></figcaption>
          <div class="cs-clock">
            <span class="cs-clock-label">Seat reservation, set by the venue</span>
            <span class="cs-clock-track"><span class="cs-clock-bar cs-clock-bar--hold">2 hours</span></span>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-clock">
            <span class="cs-clock-label">Payment reference, set by the payment provider</span>
            <span class="cs-clock-track">
              <span class="cs-clock-bar cs-clock-bar--voucher">3 days</span>
            </span>
          </div>
          <p class="cs-clock-gap">
            After the reservation ends, the customer can still pay, but the
            seats are no longer reserved for them.
          </p>
        </figure>

        <p class="cs-bridge">
          When these two time limits are not aligned, five problems can appear.
          Each problem leads to the next one.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 4 -->
      <section id="problems" aria-labelledby="problems-title">
        <h2 id="problems-title">Five problems</h2>
        {
          problems.map((problem, index) => (
            <div class="cs-point">
              <h3>
                <span class="cs-point-number">Problem {index + 1}</span>
                {problem.title}
              </h3>
              {problem.body.map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}
              <p class="cs-bridge">{problem.next}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </section>

      <!-- 5 -->
      <section id="solutions" aria-labelledby="solutions-title">
        <h2 id="solutions-title">How to solve these problems: seven steps</h2>
        <ol class="cs-steps">
          {
            solutions.map((step) => (
              <li>
                <h3>{step.title}</h3>
                <p>{step.body}</p>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
        <p class="cs-bridge">
          These seven steps reduce the number of failed orders. However, they do
          not remove the cause of the problem: the time between the order and
          the payment. The next section describes three ways to reduce this
          time.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 6 -->
      <section id="root-cause" aria-labelledby="root-title">
        <h2 id="root-title">The root cause: the time between the order and the payment</h2>
        <p>
          A cash voucher separates two moments: the moment the customer orders
          and the moment the customer pays. Between them, hours or days can
          pass. This is not a problem for a product that can wait. It is a
          problem for a product that is only reserved for a limited time. There
          are three ways to reduce this time.
        </p>

        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Option 1</span> Instant bank payments: Mexico's version of Pix</h3>
          <p>
            In Brazil, Pix confirms bank payments in a few seconds, so the
            payment happens inside the reservation time. Mexico has similar
            infrastructure. SPEI, the central bank's instant transfer system,
            processed more than 7.3 billion transfers in 2025. The central bank
            also created two simpler services on top of SPEI: CoDi (payments
            with QR codes) and DiMo (payments with a phone number).
          </p>
          <p>
            However, few people use them: less than 13% of people have used
            CoDi, and less than 7% have tried DiMo. The central bank now
            requires banks to make their transfer process more uniform by
            December 2026. Offering bank payments through SPEI at checkout is a
            good option today for customers with a bank account. It does not
            help customers without one.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-bridge">
            For customers without a bank account, a different approach is
            needed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Option 2</span> Customers add cash to a digital wallet before they order</h3>
          <p>
            This option changes the order of the steps: the customer pays in
            cash first and orders second. Digital wallets such as Spin by OXXO,
            reported to have more than 16 million users, let customers deposit
            cash at an OXXO store and then pay online immediately with a Visa
            card. The customer still visits a store, but before the order, so
            the payment and the ticket happen at the same time.
          </p>
          <p>
            The disadvantage is extra effort for the customer: they need the
            app, and they pay a small fee for each cash deposit.
          </p>
          <p class="cs-bridge">
            The third option does not change how the customer pays. It changes
            how the reservation works.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="cs-point">
          <h3><span class="cs-point-number">Option 3</span> A longer reservation, for a fee</h3>
          <p>
            A business can offer a longer reservation for a fee. Some airlines
            in Mexico already do this. Volaris, for example, lets customers pay
            a fee to keep a booking and then pay the full amount within 72
            hours. For the marketplace in this example, a small fee would pay
            for a reservation that is long enough for a visit to the store. The
            business receives money for the risk of blocking the seats, and the
            customer knows that the ticket is guaranteed.
          </p>
          <p>
            This option needs clear terms, a check of consumer protection
            rules, and agreement from the company that owns the inventory.
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="cs-compare" aria-labelledby="compare-title">
          <figcaption id="compare-title">The three options compared</figcaption>
          <div class="cs-table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Option</th>
                  <th scope="col">Who it helps</th>
                  <th scope="col">Payment speed</th>
                  <th scope="col">Main cost</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {
                  options.map((option) => (
                    <tr>
                      <th scope="row">{option.name}</th>
                      <td>{option.helps}</td>
                      <td>{option.speed}</td>
                      <td>{option.cost}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))
                }
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </figure>

        <p class="cs-bridge">
          The best choice depends on your customers and on your agreement with
          the company that owns the inventory. In many cases, a combination
          works best.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- 7 -->
      <section id="conclusion" aria-labelledby="conclusion-title">
        <h2 id="conclusion-title">Conclusion</h2>
        <p class="cs-takeaway">
          OXXO Pay helps you sell to customers who pay in cash. If your product
          is only reserved for a limited time, two rules matter most: the
          payment deadline must end before the reservation ends, and you must
          plan in advance how to return money when an order fails.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="cs-contact" aria-label="Contact">
        <p>
          Do you use a cash payment method for products with a time limit, and
          do you see orders that are paid but not completed? I can help you
          analyse the problem.
        </p>
        <div class="cs-closing-links">
          <a href={emailHref}>matteo.visibility@gmail.com</a>
          <a href="/payment-solutions/">How I help with payments →</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="cs-sources" aria-labelledby="sources-title">
        <h2 id="sources-title">Sources</h2>
        <ol>
          {
            sources.map((source) => (
              <li>
                <a href={source.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{source.name}</a>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
      </section>

      <a class="cs-back" href="/payment-solutions/case-studies/">← All case studies</a>
    </article>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
